# Regenerating @CALCULUS@

The `@CALCULUS@` package is compiled from a Eunoia signature rather than
written by hand. This directory is what does that.

```text
install/get-eo-compiler.sh      fetch and build the Eunoia compiler
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh  compile the signature into @CALCULUS@/
install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo      the signature, kept in git
install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eos     what its symbols mean, kept in git
install/defs/smt.eos            the SMT-LIB semantics that is written against
install/deps/                   the Ethos tree and the compiler, ignored by git
```

## Usage

```bash
install/get-eo-compiler.sh          # once
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh      # compile install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo
scripts/build.sh                    # check the result
```

Any signature reachable on the machine can be compiled instead, including one
being edited:

```bash
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh ~/sigs/@CALCULUS@.eo
```

`--check` installs nothing and exits 1 if the package has drifted from its
signature. Both scripts take `--help`.

## The recorded signature

A signature given by path is **recorded**: written to `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo`
as a single self-contained file, with every `(include "...")` replaced by the
text of the file it names, each file appearing once and in the order ethos
reads them, and the comments dropped.

That matters because a signature usually sits in a tree of includes and cannot
be copied on its own. The flattened copy compiles to the same Lean as the
original tree, so:

- a regeneration needs nothing outside this repository, which is what lets the
  `regeneration` CI group run anywhere;
- a diff of that file is a diff of the calculus, rather than a diff of a
  checkout.

The header records the path it was read from, and the commit if it came from a
git checkout. It deliberately does not record *which* checkout in a way that
would change the file: the copy is a function of the signature and of nothing
else, so regenerating from a different copy of the same calculus leaves it
alone. Note the commit in the message of whatever commit updates it.

`--no-record` skips this. A `--rules` run records nothing, because a reduced
calculus is not the signature.

## What is generated and what is not

Generated, and overwritten by every install:

- `@CALCULUS@/@CHECKER@.lean` — the core checker
- `@CALCULUS@/@CHECKER@Term.lean` — the term datatype
- `@CALCULUS@/Parser.lean` — the operator table for this signature
- `@CALCULUS@/SmtEval.lean`
- `@CALCULUS@/SmtModelDefs.lean`
- `@CALCULUS@/SmtValueOrder.lean`
- `@CALCULUS@/SmtModel.lean`
- `@CALCULUS@/Spec.lean`
- `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/RuleLemmas.lean` — the dispatcher over the rules

The compiler names the first two after Logos, whatever this checker is called;
the installer renames them and rewrites the imports.

Hand-written, and left alone: `@CALCULUS@/Api*.lean`,
`@CALCULUS@/Diagnostics.lean`, `@CALCULUS@/ModelWf.lean`, and everything under
`@CALCULUS@/Proofs/` other than `RuleLemmas.lean` and `Rules/`.

**Rule files are preserved.** The compiler emits each rule as its statement
with `sorry` for a proof, and the proof then lives in that same file, so an
existing file is kept and only a rule with no file yet gets a stub:

* a rule new to the signature appears as a `sorry` stub to discharge
* a rule whose *statement* changed keeps its old file and therefore fails to
  build, which is how a proof needing attention shows up — build after an
  install to find them
* a reinstall over an up-to-date tree changes nothing

## The three files a specification is

`defs/@CALCULUS@.eo` is the calculus: its sorts, operators and proof rules.
`defs/@CALCULUS@.eos` says what those symbols mean, as a transformation into
the deep embedding. `defs/smt.eos` is the SMT-LIB semantics that transformation
is written against — the same for any calculus over ordinary SMT-LIB, so a new
calculus replaces the first two and keeps the third.

Changing what the calculus means changes what a verdict of `correct` asserts,
so `Spec.lean` and the `SmtModel` modules are downstream of these files and are
regenerated from them.

## Pinning

The Ethos commit is not an option of `install-@CALCLOWER@.sh`. It is set in
`get-eo-compiler.sh`, so that what the compiler emits changes only when someone
moves it deliberately. See the note above `DEV_MODE` in that script.
